To add a toolbar in a fragment, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new Toolbar object and set it as the action bar for your activity.
2. In your fragment's onCreateView() method, inflate the layout that contains the toolbar.
3. Find the toolbar view by its ID using findViewById().
4. Set the toolbar as the support action bar for your fragment using setHasOptionsMenu(true) and onCreateOptionsMenu(Menu menu).
5. Override the onOptionsItemSelected(MenuItem item) method to handle any actions performed on the toolbar items.

Here is an example code snippet:

```java
public class MyFragment extends Fragment {

    private Toolbar toolbar;

    @Override
    public View onCreateView(LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container,
                             Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        // Inflate the layout for this fragment
        View view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_my, container, false);

        // Find the toolbar view by its ID
        toolbar = view.findViewById(R.id.toolbar);

        // Set the toolbar as the support action bar for the fragment
        ((AppCompatActivity) getActivity()).setSupportActionBar(toolbar);
        setHasOptionsMenu(true);

        return view;
    }

    @Override
    public void onCreateOptionsMenu(Menu menu, MenuInflater inflater) {
        // Inflate the menu; this adds items to the action bar if it is present.
        inflater.inflate(R.menu.my_menu, menu);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean onOptionsItemSelected(MenuItem item) {
        // Handle action bar item clicks here. The action bar will
        // automatically handle clicks on the Home/Up button, so long
        // as you specify a parent activity in AndroidManifest.xml.
        int id = item.getItemId();

        //noinspection SimplifiableIfStatement
        if (id == R.id.action_settings) {
            // Do something when settings option is clicked
            return true;
        }

        return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item);
    }
}
```

In this example, we first inflate the layout for our fragment and find the toolbar view by its ID. We then set the toolbar as the support action bar for our fragment using AppCompatActivity's setSupportActionBar() method. Finally, we override the onCreateOptionsMenu() and onOptionsItemSelected() methods to handle any actions performed on the toolbar items.

Note: Make sure to include the necessary dependencies in your build.gradle file for using the androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar library.

