Here's a simple Java program that calculates the GCD of two positive integers using the Euclidean algorithm:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GCDCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter first positive integer: ");
        int num1 = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter second positive integer: ");
        int num2 = scanner.nextInt();

        if (num1 <= 0 || num2 <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Both numbers must be positive.");
        } else {
            int gcd = calculateGCD(num1, num2);
            System.out.printf("The GCD of %d and %d is %d.%n", num1, num2, gcd);
        }

        scanner.close();
    }

    private static int calculateGCD(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int temp = b;
            b = a % b;
            a = temp;
        }
        return a;
    }
}
```

This program uses the `Scanner` class to read input from the user. It then checks if both entered numbers are positive; if not, it prints an error message. If they are positive, it calls the `calculateGCD()` method with these numbers as arguments and stores the result in the variable `gcd`. Finally, it prints out the calculated GCD.

The `calculateGCD()` method implements the Euclidean algorithm for finding the GCD of two numbers. The algorithm works by repeatedly replacing the larger number with its remainder when divided by the smaller number until one of them becomes zero. At this point, the other number will be the GCD.

